WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.501
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:09.048
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.348 --> 00:00:16.912
Unit five: Innovation Management. Today you will describe innovation processes with before / after sequencing clauses.

4
00:00:17.212 --> 00:00:19.701
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:23.900
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:25.786
Innovation.

7
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:30.896
Innovation means a new idea, product or method that creates value.

8
00:00:31.483 --> 00:00:35.015
Mobile QR payments were a major innovation for street vendors.

9
00:00:35.617 --> 00:00:38.199
Your turn. Say: Innovation.

10
00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:42.151
Innovation.

11
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:43.964
R&D.

12
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:49.539
R&D means research and development; work to create new products.

13
00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.554
The firm invests 8% of revenue in R&D.

14
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:57.000
Your turn. Say: R&D.

15
00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:01.624
R&D.

16
00:01:02.383 --> 00:01:04.174
Disruptive technology.

17
00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:09.876
Disruptive technology means a technology that overturns an established industry.

18
00:01:10.483 --> 00:01:14.445
Ride-hailing apps were a disruptive technology for tuk-tuk drivers.

19
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.747
Your turn. Say: Disruptive technology.

20
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.541
Disruptive technology.

21
00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:25.319
Prototype.

22
00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:29.582
Prototype means an early model built to test an idea.

23
00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:33.344
The team built a cardboard prototype in one afternoon.

24
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.614
Your turn. Say: Prototype.

25
00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:40.421
Prototype.

26
00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:42.219
Iteration.

27
00:01:43.217 --> 00:01:46.772
Iteration means one cycle of testing and improving a design.

28
00:01:47.383 --> 00:01:51.333
After the fifth iteration, users finally understood the menu.

29
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.649
Your turn. Say: Iteration.

30
00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.478
Iteration.

31
00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:00.322
Market fit.

32
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:05.336
Market fit means the degree to which a product satisfies real market demand.

33
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.575
Strong market fit shows up as customers recommending the product.

34
00:02:10.183 --> 00:02:12.975
Your turn. Say: Market fit.

35
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:16.916
Market fit.

36
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.046
Scalability.

37
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.313
Scalability means the ability to grow without costs rising at the same rate.

38
00:02:24.917 --> 00:02:28.565
Software has far greater scalability than handmade goods.

39
00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.721
Your turn. Say: Scalability.

40
00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:35.765
Scalability.

41
00:02:36.517 --> 00:02:37.622
Ideation.

42
00:02:38.617 --> 00:02:41.824
Ideation means the process of generating new ideas.

43
00:02:42.417 --> 00:02:45.577
Ideation sessions produced over a hundred suggestions.

44
00:02:46.183 --> 00:02:48.824
Your turn. Say: Ideation.

45
00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:52.807
Ideation.

46
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.146
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:57.983 --> 00:03:04.479
Visal, your team wants to launch a farming app across all twenty-five provinces next month. Isn't that rushed?

48
00:03:05.583 --> 00:03:14.389
It might be. Before scaling, we should test market fit with farmers in one province, after the rice harvest when they have time.

49
00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:17.367
What will you learn from that pilot?

50
00:03:18.483 --> 00:03:25.803
Whether farmers actually use it. Only after observing real users can we know which features matter and which are noise.

51
00:03:26.917 --> 00:03:28.707
And if the first version fails?

52
00:03:29.817 --> 00:03:39.377
Then we iterate. Each prototype should be tested before more money is spent; failing early is far cheaper than failing nationally.

53
00:03:40.533 --> 00:03:42.022
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.883 --> 00:03:46.148
Grammar focus: Before and after sequencing clauses.

55
00:03:46.448 --> 00:03:47.693
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:48.383 --> 00:03:50.940
Market fit must be tested before scaling.

57
00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:55.440
Only after observing real users can we choose features.

58
00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.394
The team refined the design after the first pilot failed.

59
00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:10.219
'Before' and 'after' can be followed by a clause (after the pilot failed) or an -ing form (before scaling). 'Only after…' at the start of a sentence requires inversion: Only after testing can we…

60
00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:15.099
Be careful! Don't say: Only after testing we can launch.

61
00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:19.927
Say: Only after testing can we launch.

62
00:04:20.227 --> 00:04:21.855
'Only after…' needs inversion.

63
00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:25.273
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:27.997
Fail Small, Learn Fast.

65
00:04:28.297 --> 00:04:34.476
Innovation management is the discipline of turning creative ideas into products that people actually use.

66
00:04:35.467 --> 00:04:42.563
Modern practice favours rapid prototyping, in which rough versions are tested with real users before large sums are invested.

67
00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:49.456
Each iteration reveals assumptions that looked sensible on paper but collapse in practice.

68
00:04:50.467 --> 00:04:56.344
Only after a product shows clear market fit should a company focus on scalability and mass marketing.

69
00:04:57.333 --> 00:05:04.186
Firms that skip these steps often discover, too late, that they have built an impressive solution to a problem nobody has.

70
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.348
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:10.183 --> 00:05:12.763
We should test the idea before blank more money.

72
00:05:13.063 --> 00:05:17.088
A, invest. B, investing. C, invested.

73
00:05:20.633 --> 00:05:23.109
The answer is B: investing.

74
00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:27.260
An early model built to test an idea is a blank.

75
00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.258
A, prototype. B, scalability. C, market fit.

76
00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.927
The answer is A: prototype.

77
00:05:39.083 --> 00:05:42.383
One cycle of testing and improving a design is an blank.

78
00:05:42.683 --> 00:05:47.184
A, ideation. B, R&D. C, iteration.

79
00:05:50.733 --> 00:05:52.837
The answer is C: iteration.

80
00:05:53.983 --> 00:05:55.727
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:56.027 --> 00:06:03.338
A, Only after testing we can launch.. B, Only after testing can we launch.. C, Only after test can we launch..

82
00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:10.281
The answer is B: Only after testing can we launch..

83
00:06:11.417 --> 00:06:14.670
According to the reading, firms that skip testing may build…

84
00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:21.143
A, a solution to a problem nobody has. B, cheaper products. C, faster factories.

85
00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:28.267
The answer is A: a solution to a problem nobody has.

86
00:06:29.467 --> 00:06:32.862
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:33.717 --> 00:06:37.806
What is the value of rapid iteration when developing something new?

88
00:06:54.617 --> 00:06:56.163
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:56.463 --> 00:07:34.197
Rapid iteration is valuable because it exposes mistakes while they are still cheap to fix. Before investing heavily, a team can build a simple prototype, watch real users and change the design within days. For example, a Cambodian delivery app might discover after a small pilot that customers prefer paying cash, which would completely change its plans. Only after several iterations does a product usually fit the market well. In my view, learning quickly matters far more than being perfect at the first attempt.

90
00:07:34.497 --> 00:07:36.230
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:37.117 --> 00:07:42.149
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and before and after sequencing clauses.

92
00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:47.447
Today's value is innovation. Try new ideas boldly, and learn from every attempt.

93
00:07:47.747 --> 00:07:50.364
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:50.664 --> 00:07:53.154
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

